{"id":"ltc-related-balance-history","title":"The 42 homes owed most by related companies: how the balances built up, 2012 to 2025","question":"For the Bay Area homes whose latest report shows half or more of their assets owed by related companies, when did those balances appear and how fast did they grow?","finding":"The 42 homes (owed $1,021.5 million at their latest report) were owed $318.0 million in 2012, $235.3 million in 2015, $371.9 million in 2018, $476.5 million in 2020, $503.4 million in 2021 and $717.2 million in 2024; the total rose 42% in the last year alone. 14 of them already met the test (at least half of assets and at least $3 million) in 2015 or earlier; 7 met it first in 2022 or later. The three San Francisco homes (each naming PACS as a related party from 2020 on) went from -$1.5 million in 2015 to $16.6 million in 2020 and $63.4 million in 2025: the balance grew by $46.8 million after 2020, against $49.9 million of net income the three reported in 2021 to 2025. Across the 41 homes with a 2020 report, the balance grew by $523.3 million after 2020 against $558.9 million of net income; for 25 of them the growth was within a quarter of the income. The 42 homes listed related-party transactions of $76.3 million in 2020 and $75.0 million in 2021 ($40.8 million in 2015, $72.3 million in 2025).","how":"Homes chosen exactly as in the nursing-home case (Bay Area skilled-nursing licences with a 2024 or 2025 report; net amount owed by related parties at least half of total assets and at least $3 million). For each year the net amount is current plus non-current receivables from related parties less payables to related parties (report page 5.1 lines 50 and 160, page 5.2 lines 45 and 110), at the end of the last report filed that year. 2015 to 2025 come from the complete data sets; 2012 to 2014 from the pivot profiles, which carry the same four balance-sheet lines (REL_REC_CR, REL_REC_LT, REL_PAY_CR, REL_PAY_LT). A negative payable is counted as owed, as in the case.","limits":"A balance owed by an affiliate is often a cash sweep: the chain collects each home's cash centrally and books it as owed back, so the balance rises with the profit the home leaves with the chain instead of paying it out as a dividend. It is not a payment, it is not a finding that money was lost, and it may be repaid or offset at a sale. A home that changed operator may report under a new State number, so a blank year can mean a different licensee rather than no balance. 2023 to 2025 are unaudited.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"city","t":"City","type":"text"},{"k":"b2012","t":"2012","type":"usd"},{"k":"b2015","t":"2015","type":"usd"},{"k":"b2018","t":"2018","type":"usd"},{"k":"b2020","t":"2020","type":"usd"},{"k":"b2021","t":"2021","type":"usd"},{"k":"bLatest","t":"Latest report","type":"usd"},{"k":"latestYear","t":"Latest year","type":"year"},{"k":"first50","t":"First year at half of assets","type":"year"},{"k":"share2015","t":"Share of assets, 2015","type":"pct"},{"k":"growth2020","t":"Growth since 2020","type":"usd"},{"k":"niSince2020","t":"Net income, 2021 to latest","type":"usd"},{"k":"growthToIncome","t":"Growth per dollar of net income","type":"times"}],"rows":[{"growth2020":67396083,"niSince2020":35838121,"growthToIncome":1.88,"home":"Terraces of Los Gatos","city":"Los Gatos","county":"Santa Clara","b2012":40861932,"b2015":56307188,"b2018":77857264,"b2020":93942903,"b2021":109244828,"bLatest":161338986,"latestYear":2025,"first50":2012,"share2015":0.571,"yearsOnFile":14},{"growth2020":17132370,"niSince2020":20873520,"growthToIncome":0.82,"home":"Piedmont Gardens Health Facility","city":"Oakland","county":"Alameda","b2012":36079130,"b2015":42460953,"b2018":45920498,"b2020":49843331,"b2021":50550611,"bLatest":66975701,"latestYear":2025,"first50":2012,"share2015":0.796,"yearsOnFile":14},{"growth2020":21391149,"niSince2020":11866895,"growthToIncome":1.8,"home":"Pleasanton Nursing and Rehabilitat